{
  "dom_id": "step-e744",
  "name": "Example: Obtaining a Higher-Order Derivative Of a Function Given its Higher-Degree Taylor Polynomial",
  "problem": "The fourth-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nP_4(x) = 1+2x-3x^2+4x^3-5x^4.\n$$\n\nWhat is $f^{(4)}(0)?$",
  "worked_solution": "The coefficient of $x^4$ is given by $\\dfrac{f^{(4)}(0)}{4!}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{(4)}(0)}{4!} & =-5 \\\\ \\frac{{f}^{(4)}(0)}{24} & =-5 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(0) & =-120.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
